WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.965
E58 showed the v86 Linux VM as a viewer feature.

2
00:00:01.965 --> 00:00:07.000
This episode reopens it as a developer — the source, the design decisions, and the actual commit history behind landing it.

3
00:00:07.000 --> 00:00:07.121
vm.

4
00:00:07.121 --> 00:00:14.289
ts talks to v86 through one narrow interface: add_listener for serial bytes coming out of the guest, serial0_send for typing into it, and create_file/read_file for the 9p bridge.

5
00:00:14.289 --> 00:00:19.000
Everything else in the file is built on just those four calls — deliberately narrow, so v86 itself stays a black box.

6
00:00:19.000 --> 00:00:20.926
The guest only talks back one byte at a time over serial, so vm.

7
00:00:20.926 --> 00:00:24.928
ts turns that into something awaitable: onSerialByte appends every byte to a running buffer and checks it against a list of watchers.

8
00:00:24.928 --> 00:00:28.750
waitFor registers one — a test function and a timeout — and resolves the moment the buffer satisfies it, or rejects on timeout.

9
00:00:28.750 --> 00:00:32.000
That's how bootVM waits for the shell prompt, and how every command run waits for its own completion marker.

10
00:00:32.000 --> 00:00:36.342
The 9p bridge is what makes this more than a toy: the guest mounts a share at slash mnt, and the host's create_file with a workspace-prefixed path appears there.

11
00:00:36.342 --> 00:00:43.031
So GUEST_WORKDIR is slash mnt slash workspace, and before every command pushWorkspace walks the OPFS tree and copies each file in over that bridge; after the command, pullWorkspace runs find in the guest and copies anything new or changed back out.

12
00:00:43.031 --> 00:00:45.000
The file tools and a real Linux command end up looking at the same files.

13
00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:48.526
Only one command name is registered for all of this: run_command, the same name the coreutils shell shim in shell.

14
00:00:48.526 --> 00:00:49.021
ts also answers.

15
00:00:49.021 --> 00:00:51.093
The router is a Settings toggle read from localStorage — vmEnabled.

16
00:00:51.093 --> 00:00:54.371
On, it tries the VM and falls back to the shell if boot fails; off, it goes straight to the instant shell.

17
00:00:54.371 --> 00:00:57.000
One invoke call site in the app, two completely different implementations underneath.

18
00:00:57.000 --> 00:01:02.976
And here's how it actually landed, in order, in the real history: first a shell scaffold — a coreutils subset over the same OPFS workspace, shipping something useful immediately.

19
00:01:02.976 --> 00:01:05.830
Then a VM milestone that only boots and runs a raw command, no filesystem bridge yet.

20
00:01:05.830 --> 00:01:08.851
Then a second VM milestone that wires the 9p bridge in and switches run_command's routing.

21
00:01:08.851 --> 00:01:11.000
Three separate, independently useful commits, not one giant one.

22
00:01:11.000 --> 00:01:15.370
The lesson worth taking from this into your own contribution: each of those three commits worked and shipped value on its own.

23
00:01:15.370 --> 00:01:16.757
The shell alone was a complete terminal.

24
00:01:16.757 --> 00:01:17.936
The VM that only boots was a demo.

25
00:01:17.936 --> 00:01:23.000
Only the third commit needed the other two — and by then the risky, novel part (does v86 even boot reliably) had already been proven in isolation.

26
00:01:23.000 --> 00:01:24.624
You've now read a real feature end to end.

27
00:01:24.624 --> 00:01:30.000
Time to build one yourself — a small command, implemented on both sides of the seam, run from both the desktop build and the browser build.

